      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright 2010 American Congress of Rehabilitation Medicine. Published by Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
            "content": "To investigate the reliability and validity of 2 commonly used measures of pain related fear in patients with shoulder pain."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "DESIGN",
            "content": "A preplanned secondary analysis of a prospective single-arm trial involving a repeated-measures design."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "SETTING",
            "content": "Outpatient physical therapy clinics."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "PARTICIPANTS",
            "content": "Patients (N=80) with a primary report of shoulder pain."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "INTERVENTION",
            "content": "All patients completed the outcome measures at baseline and at follow-up."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES",
            "content": "Patients completed a modified Fear-Avoidance Beliefs Questionnaire (FABQ), the 11-item version of Tampa Scale of Kinesiophobia (TSK-11), and the Shoulder Pain and Disability Index (SPADI) at baseline and at a 48-hour follow-up. Patients were dichotomized as improved or stable at follow-up based on the Global Rating of Change."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Factor analysis indicated 3 stable factors for the FABQ and 1 stable factor for the TSK-11. Shoulder specific scoring for the FABQ and TSK-11 were used in subsequent analyses. Test-retest reliability intraclass correlation coefficient (ICC) was substantial for the FABQ and the TSK-11. The FABQ correlated significantly with SPADI pain and disability scores, while the TSK-11 correlated significantly only with SPADI pain scores. The shoulder-specific FABQ-W (work beliefs subscale) was a better than chance predictor of missing days of work during the 48-hour study period."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "The modified FABQ and TSK-11 may be appropriate for use in patients with shoulder pain. Shoulder-specific scoring of these measures resulted in substantial test-retest reliability, and the FABQ correlated with the SPADI for pain and disability. The FABQ also showed potential for prediction of short-term work loss in this sample. Pain-related fear may be an important variable in patients with shoulder pain and merits future consideration in longitudinal studies."
          }
        ]
      },
